   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on in the body",
   "text": "A paper reports that GM1 gangliosidosis is caused by changes (mutations) in a gene called GLB1 [Ede0cb24, E8207ea3]. A paper reports that this gene normally makes an enzyme, a helper protein that breaks things down, named beta-galactosidase, and the changes stop it from clipping a sugar piece called galactose off a fatty substance [E7b71018]. A paper reports that without enough working enzyme, the body cannot break down certain fats and sugars, so they build up inside the cell's recycling parts, called lysosomes [Eb8a3f15, E995e5af]. A paper reports that one of the substances that builds up is a fatty sugar called a glycosphingolipid, which collects in nerve tissue [Ecc4ff41]. A paper reports that this build-up is linked with a loss of nerve cells in the brain, called neurodegeneration [Ecba944f].",
   "citations": [
