   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases that share the same biology",
   "text": "A paper reports that Niemann-Pick A, like GM1, is caused by a missing or low-working lysosomal enzyme [E995e5af, E9c78c68]. According to Reactome, the genes behind both diseases take part in the same body process for breaking down fatty sugars, called glycosphingolipid metabolism [E4074b83, E41520eb, Eacfbed3]. According to HPO, both GM1 and Niemann-Pick A are listed with a red spot at the back of the eye called a cherry red spot of the macula, an infant start (infantile onset), and a delay in reaching childhood milestones (global developmental delay) [E86d0e19, E7792b85, Ed346610, Eabe7635, E6759d28, Ecdbe34d]. A paper reports that Tay-Sachs is also caused by a missing lysosomal enzyme, like GM1 [E87925e8]. According to HPO, Tay-Sachs and GM1 are both listed with increased muscle stiffness called hypertonia, a cherry red spot of the macula, and an infant start [E2b5c257, E3527e1a, E86d0e19, E933ccf3, Ed346610, Ec6feaac]. A paper reports that MPS IIID, also called Sanfilippo syndrome type D, is caused by a missing lysosomal enzyme too [Ef8f5811]. According to HPO, GM1 and MPS IIID are both listed with flattened bones in the spine (hypoplastic vertebral bodies), a broad forehead (frontal bossing), and wide-set eyes (hypertelorism) [E8a2584e, Ec8ec6f3, Ecbc0fc7, E68f3328, E6f6bcb3, Ef39a105].",
