   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "Research, trials and groups that exist",
   "text": "According to ClinicalTrials.gov, a gene therapy study giving an AAV9 vector carrying the beta-galactosidase gene is recruiting in the United States [E66ca8c7, E77cb86a, Ed9ba6e0, E6df8f20, E4b7b211, E9fabf02, E7239626]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, further studies are testing an oral medicine called Nizubaglustat (AZ-3102) for GM1 and related conditions, listed across many countries, with some recruiting and one no longer recruiting [E118ac08, E05ee7b4, E93e7b0e, E975cb48, E1c38ce1, E494d62f]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, a natural history study called 'Natural History of Glycosphingolipid Storage Disorders and Glycoprotein Disorders' is recruiting in the United States [Ec81c2d2, Ed665e20]. A paper reports that the National Tay-Sachs & Allied Diseases Association, based in the United States, works to support and research Tay-Sachs, Canavan, GM1, and Sandhoff diseases [E3f52bb3]. A paper reports that the Cure GM1 Foundation, based in the United States, is a nonprofit organization dedicated to GM1 research and drug development [E0516da3]. A paper reports that the MPS Society, based in Great Britain, works on support, research, and awareness for conditions including GM1 [E18b167e]. According to NIH RePORTER, NIH NINDS and NIH NIDDK fund several research grants studying GM1, including gene therapy work and mouse models of the disease [E8018779, Eacd0141, Eba5a545, Eeb25e7d, E823e7fd, Eef783f2]. Cramer, Tianhong Yang, Walter Acosta, and Tippi Mackenzie are listed as leading some of these GM1 research grants [E5894b74, Ed59f4b9, E73d2169, Eefe7dac, E28a6fee].",
